  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Keeping The Seattle Times under family control shaped Clarance B. Blethen’s agreement with the Ridder brothers in 1929. He exchanged his existing interest for a majority of the new company’s voting shares, while the Ridders supplied capital. The agreement combined outside investment with safeguards designed to preserve his family’s authority over the newspaper.\n\nBlethen also commissioned a new headquarters, completed in 1931 and designed by architect Robert Reamer. Its site lay close to Seattle’s business district but offered space for further expansion. The complex separated an ornamental office frontage from the more utilitarian printing plant, giving the growing newspaper room for both editorial work and mechanical production.",
    "html": "<p>Keeping The Seattle Times under family control shaped Clarance B. Blethen’s agreement with the Ridder brothers in 1929. He exchanged his existing interest for a majority of the new company’s voting shares, while the Ridders supplied capital. The agreement combined outside investment with safeguards designed to preserve his family’s authority over the newspaper.</p>\r\n<p>Blethen also commissioned a new headquarters, completed in 1931 and designed by architect Robert Reamer. Its site lay close to Seattle’s business district but offered space for further expansion. The complex separated an ornamental office frontage from the more utilitarian printing plant, giving the growing newspaper room for both editorial work and mechanical production.</p>\r\n",
